data: {"type":"tool-output-available","toolCallId":"call-db36f2bf-e8dc-4685-8a68-75acc9b0f36c","output":{"output":"<repo>github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs</repo>\n<path>content/en-US/docs/app-shell.mdx</path>\n<url>undefined</url>\n<content>\n50: ### setupApps()\n51: \n52: The `setupApps` function loads all of the routes from the routes registry. It reads routes from:\n53: - `<script type=\"openmrs-routes\">` tags in the HTML (either inline JSON or from a URL)\n54: - Route overrides stored in `localStorage` (useful for development and debugging). These are only read when the app shell runs in development mode.\n55: \n56: After loading all routes, it invokes `registerApp()` on each module and its route definition. The route definitions stay available on `window.installedModules`, which lets tools inspect module versions and backend dependency metadata. `registerApp()` itself then:\n57: \n58: - Registers an implicit configuration schema for the application.\n59: - Iterates through the extensions and registers them with the extension registry.\n60: - Iterates through modals, workspaces, workspace groups, workspace windows, and feature flags and registers them.\n61: - Pages get added to a global array. After all modules are registered, `finishRegisteringAllApps` sorts pages alphabetically by app name, then creates a `<div>` element for each page in the DOM. The div is created inside the container specified by the page's `containerDomId` property (defaults to `omrs-apps-container`). This ensures that single-spa has a predetermined DOM element to mount each page into, which is required for proper routing behavior.\n62: \n63: Pages and extensions are implemented as Single Spa objects, which are essentially JavaScript objects that define three lifecycle functions:\n64: \n65: - `bootstrap` - This function is called once (and only once) when the application is first loaded. It's responsible for loading any dependencies the application needs.\n66: - `mount` - This function is called when the page is first loaded. It's responsible for rendering the page. Under the hood, this typically calls `ReactDOM.createRoot()` and renders the React tree into the DOM element that corresponds to the page.\n67: - `unmount` - This function is called when the page is unloaded. It's responsible for cleaning up any resources the page is using.\n68: \n69: Both pages and extensions are loaded using the `loadLifeCycles()` function which takes the app name and component name and returns a Promise that resolves to the single-spa lifecycle object. If you look at a frontend module's entry point (`src/index.ts`), you'll see named exports that invoke either the `getAsyncLifecycle` or `getSyncLifecycle` function. These functions allow us to wrap pages and extensions into a format that can be loaded by single-spa. Essentially, the React component gets wrapped in an `openmrsComponentDecorator` that allows the framework to:\n70: \n71: - Handle errors if the rendering fails catastrophically.\n72: - Wire up configuration support for the component.\n73: - Wire up `i18n` support for the component.\n74: - Wire up a Suspense fallback for the component that defaults to `null`.\n75: - Render the component.\n76: \n77: When you define a `page`, the page definition can have a `route` property or a `routeRegex` property. Single-spa uses the location referenced by the `route` or `routeRegex` to determine the pages that get rendered into that location via the `getActivityFn` function. Pages in O3 are essentially single-spa applications with a predetermined `<div>` tag that they get rendered into. Extensions leverage the single-spa `parcel` concept - they have the exact same lifecycles as single-spa applications, except that they don't have a `getActivityFn`. This means single-spa will never automatically mount or unmount a parcel. Instead, you have to manually tell it when to mount or unmount a parcel. The extension system exists to determine when an extension should be loaded (and subsequently invoke the `mount` function on it) and unloaded (and subsequently invoke the `unmount` function on it). Every extension is mounted through the `Extension` component defined in the framework. This component essentially renders the extension (by invoking single-spa's `mount` function) and unmounts the parcel when the component is unmounted. It defines a `<div>` with a `data-extension-id` property into which React renders the parcel. The key thing to note about pages and extensions is that once `<div>`s get created for them, React takes over and renders the page or extension into the DOM.\n78: \n79: ## Module loading\n80: \n81: O3 loads frontend modules using import maps and Module Federation. The app shell includes one or more\n82: `systemjs-importmap` script tags in `index.ejs`, but the current runtime reads those tags itself through the\n83: dynamic-loading package. SystemJS is no longer the piece that imports module code at runtime.\n84: \n85: For each module URL, the app shell appends a `<script>` tag. When the script loads, it exposes a Module Federation\n86: container on `window` using the module name transformed into a JavaScript identifier. The app shell then calls the\n87: container's `init` and `get` functions to load the module's `./start` export.\n88: \n89: When a page, extension, modal, or workspace is about to render, `loadLifeCycles()` imports the owning frontend module,\n\n(Showing lines 50-89 of 153. Use offset=90 to continue.)\n</content>","sources":[{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/app-shell.mdx","name":"app-shell.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}]}}

